---
/**
 * Gum Contouring — Vivid Smiles Dentistry, Parker CO.
 *
 * Template: src/pages/cosmetic-dentistry/porcelain-veneers.astro (the canonical
 * service detail layout). No the design prototype
 * exists, so the veneers page is the closest visual reference by sitewide convention.
 *
 * Page-scoped styles: src/styles/pages/gum-contouring.css.
 * Buttons + scroll reveals wired by src/scripts/animations.js (data-vs-arrow / data-vs-reveal*).
 *
 * Copy: verbatim from supplied SEO markdown ("Laser Gum Contouring in Parker, CO").
 *
 * Sections vs. veneers template:
 *  Kept: Hero · Keys · TOC · What · Laser (re-uses Natural-with-quote pattern,
 *  on a sage band) · Candidates (Why-grid 2-card) · Process · Recovery
 *  (Lasting 2-col with stat) · Results (Natural-style 2-col, no quote, alt-flip)
 *  · Why (3-up 6-card grid) · FAQ · VirtualConsult · Final band.
 *
 *  Skipped (no copy support, see plan):
 *  - Compare    — SEO source has no comparison block (the contouring-vs-grafting
 *                 distinction lives in a single FAQ, kept there).
 *  - Local      — Parker-resident + 300+-reviews moments are bullets inside the
 *                 "Why Choose Vivid Smiles" cards; a dedicated Local section
 *                 would duplicate copy.
 *  - Gallery    — no before/after gum-contouring images on disk; reusing veneer
 *                 smile tiles would violate the "never fake the categorization"
 *                 sitewide rule.
 *
 * Image strategy: only `services/gum_contouring.webp` is a real gum-contouring
 * photo on disk (used in the hero). Inner image slots reuse images from
 * porcelain-veneers/ and home/ as visible placeholders, each marked with a
 * TODO so they're easy to grep and swap when real photos arrive.
 *
 * Global <Nav /> + <Footer /> are mounted by BaseLayout — this page renders
 * only its own content, starting with an inline breadcrumb under the global nav.
 *
 * MIGRATED TO BLOCKS (docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md 2.3). Eight top-level bands — what,
 * laser, candidates, process, recovery, results, why, faq — each guarded with
 * `hasBlocks ? null : …` and each carrying a row in cms/import/block-map.json
 * under /cosmetic-dentistry/gum-contouring/. Eight `sections` rows, eight
 * `toc_links` rows, eight bands: this page has no code_section, no gallery and
 * no cost band, so the block list is the section list in template order.
 *
 * THE BAND COLOURS IN THE SECTION COMMENTS BELOW WERE WRONG AND ARE FIXED.
 * `#what` said "(cream)" and `#laser` said "(sage)"; gum-contouring.css:245 is
 * `.section.alt { background: var(--vs-charcoal-green) }` and :254 is
 * `.section.dark { background: var(--vs-sage-pale) }`. The map's `band` values
 * are read off those two rules, not off the class names and not off a sibling
 * sheet — `.section.alt` is cream on teeth-whitening.css and charcoal here.
 *
 * Three things this page loses on migration, all recorded as known_gaps in the
 * map rather than papered over here:
 *   1. `#laser` ships a `.natural-quote-attrib` byline under its pull quote and
 *      media_split has a `quote` field but no attribution field.
 *   2. `#results` is `.natural-grid.alt-flip` — the natural family mirrored —
 *      and media_split ties the class family to `media_side`, so it comes back
 *      as `.what-grid.ratio-wide-media`: same side, same column split, a 5/4
 *      plate instead of a 4/5 one.
 *   3. `#why` is `.why-3up`, not `.why-grid`, and it ends in a `.cta-row` that
 *      card_grid has no fields for.
 */
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Button from "../../components/Button.astro";
import FinalBand from "../../components/FinalBand.astro";
import VirtualConsult from "../../components/VirtualConsult.astro";
import {
  phoneHref,
  phoneLabel,
  phoneE164,
  bookNowHref,
  directionsHref,
  addressStreet,
  addressCity,
  addressState,
  addressZip,
} from "../../data/contact";
import PageBlocks from "../../blocks/PageBlocks.astro";
import "../../styles/pages/gum-contouring.css";

// "What is gum contouring" — real macro of a smile showing the gum line
// framing the upper teeth, which is the ratio this section explains.
// "Laser technology" section — macro of the gumline where it meets the teeth.
// "Results" — real patient portrait in the office. Distinct subject from
// the hero portrait; the alt describes the photograph, not an outcome.
import { getPageContent, type TocLink } from '../../lib/page-content';
// tocLinks, processSteps, faqs are edited in WordPress (Pages -> Laser Gum Contouring in Parker, CO).
// The shapes are unchanged from the arrays that used to live here, so the
// markup below is untouched.
const { blocks, hasBlocks, tocLinks, processSteps, faqs, section, image, seo } =
  await getPageContent(Astro.url.pathname);


const whyCards = [
  { tag: "Digital Preview",   title: "Digital smile preview",                       body: "Before any work is scheduled, you see a preview of your projected gum line. This is a specific, patient-facing view of your planned outcome, not a generic illustration of general improvements." },
  { tag: "Laser Technology",  title: "Laser over scalpel",                          body: "Soft tissue laser technology means less discomfort, minimal bleeding, no sutures in most cases, and significantly faster healing compared to traditional gum surgery." },
];

/**
 * The "On this page" rail, derived from the blocks themselves (docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md 1.2).
 *
 * Used only when this page renders from `blocks`. The `toc_links` repeater is
 * eight rows that restate the order of the eight bands below it, so a reorder
 * in wp-admin moves a band and leaves its rail entry pointing at the old
 * neighbour — the single largest way a reorder breaks a link. Reading the
 * anchors off the blocks makes that unrepresentable: there is one ordering and
 * the rail is a projection of it.
 *
 * Three rules, in the order they bite:
 *
 * 1. A blank anchor is skipped. Not tidiness — src/scripts/toc-spy.ts:24 feeds
 *    each rendered href straight to document.querySelector(), and
 *    querySelector("") throws a SyntaxError that kills the whole spy, not just
 *    the one entry. PageBlocks.astro emits no id at all for a blank anchor
 *    (`id={anchor || undefined}`), so there is nothing to link to either.
 * 2. An anchor that is not a valid id selector is skipped for the same reason —
 *    "#3-reasons" is legal HTML and an illegal CSS selector. Dropping one rail
 *    entry costs one link; letting it through costs every link on the page,
 *    plus the mobile pill auto-centering.
 * 3. Duplicate anchors follow PageBlocks.astro's dedup exactly, second
 *    occurrence onward suffixed `-2`, `-3`. The counter has to advance on every
 *    non-blank anchor, including blocks with no nav_label, or the rail's idea
 *    of "the second #faq" drifts from the id the renderer actually emitted.
 *
 * A blank nav_label means "leave this block out of the rail" (1.2), which is
 * how a band that should not be navigable opts out.
 */
const ANCHOR_IS_ID_SELECTOR = /^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_-]*$/;

const anchorsSeen = new Map<string, number>();
// Typed as TocLink[] rather than an equivalent inline shape so the ternary in
// the rail below collapses to one type instead of a union of two array types,
// which TypeScript will not let you call .map() on.
const derivedTocLinks: TocLink[] = [];

for (const block of blocks) {
  // A nested block is drawn INSIDE the band above it, not as a band of its own,
  // so it has no id to link to. Without this the rail emits an entry pointing at
  // an anchor nothing renders — a dead link in the "On this page" list.
  if (block?.nested) continue;

  const anchor = typeof block.anchor === 'string' ? block.anchor.trim() : '';
  if (anchor === '') continue;

  const nth = (anchorsSeen.get(anchor) ?? 0) + 1;
  anchorsSeen.set(anchor, nth);

  const label = typeof block.navLabel === 'string' ? block.navLabel.trim() : '';
  if (label === '') continue;

  if (!ANCHOR_IS_ID_SELECTOR.test(anchor)) {
    console.warn(
      `[blocks] anchor "${anchor}" on ${Astro.url.pathname} cannot be used as a selector — ` +
        `omitting "${label}" from the On this page rail so toc-spy keeps working`,
    );
    continue;
  }

  derivedTocLinks.push({ href: `#${nth > 1 ? `${anchor}-${nth}` : anchor}`, label });
}


---

<BaseLayout
  title={seo.title || "Laser Gum Contouring in Parker, CO | Vivid Smiles Dentistry"}
  description={seo.description || "Laser gum contouring in Parker, CO with Dr. Bryce Richardson. Reshape excess or uneven gum tissue with soft-tissue laser technology — minimal recovery, immediate results, permanent change."}
 noindex={seo.noindex}>
  <div class="gcontouring">

    {/* ---------- BREADCRUMB ---------- */}
    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <div class="crumbs-inner">
        <a href="/">Home</a>
        <span class="sep">/</span>
        <a href="/services/">Services</a>
        <span class="sep">/</span>
        <a href="/cosmetic-dentistry/">Cosmetic Dentistry</a>
        <span class="sep">/</span>
        <span class="here">Gum Contouring</span>
      </div>
    </nav>

    <main id="main">

    {/* ---------- HERO ---------- */}
    <header class="hero" data-vs-reveal-batch>
      <div class="wrap hero-inner">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <span class="eyebrow">Cosmetic Dentistry · Parker, CO</span>
          <h1 data-vs-reveal-lines>Laser Gum Contouring <em>in Parker, CO</em></h1>
          <p class="hero-sub">
            Gum contouring is a cosmetic dental procedure that reshapes excess or uneven gum tissue to reveal more
            of the natural tooth structure underneath. Laser technology makes the process precise and minimally
            invasive, with most patients experiencing little to no downtime. Here is who benefits, what the
            procedure involves, and how Dr. Bryce Richardson at Vivid Smiles Dentistry approaches gum reshaping
            as part of a complete smile design plan.
          </p>
          <div class="hero-actions">
            <Button variant="light" href={bookNowHref} hoverLabel="Let's Talk">Book Online</Button>
            <Button variant="ghost-light" href="#consult" hoverLabel="Get a Video">Free Virtual Consult</Button>
            <div class="ratings">
              <span class="stars" aria-hidden="true">★★★★★</span>
              <span>5.0 · 300+ reviews</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-img" data-vs-parallax data-parallax="-30">
          <Image
            src={image("heroImg").url} width={image("heroImg").width} height={image("heroImg").height}
            alt="Smiling woman photographed outdoors among Colorado pines"
            widths={[600, 900, 1400]}
            sizes="(max-width: 991px) 100vw, 50vw"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    {/* ---------- KEYS — at-a-glance ---------- */}
    <section class="keys" id="keys" aria-label="Gum contouring at a glance" data-vs-reveal-batch>
      <div class="keys-grid">
        <div class="keys-label">At a glance</div>
        <div class="keys-item"><div class="k">Laser-Precise</div><div class="v">Soft Tissue Laser</div></div>
        <div class="keys-item"><div class="k">30–60 min</div><div class="v">Procedure Time</div></div>
        <div class="keys-item"><div class="k">Same Day</div><div class="v">Return to Activities</div></div>
        <div class="keys-item"><div class="k">Permanent</div><div class="v">No Regrowth</div></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {
      /* ---------- ON-THIS-PAGE TOC ----------
       The rail itself is never a block (spec 7: "Not a block, even though it
       is a band") — only its contents move. Derived from the blocks when they
       are drawing the page, read from the `toc_links` repeater when the
       template is. Same markup either way, which is what lets
       src/scripts/toc-spy.ts go on reading its section list off the rendered
       links without knowing which source produced them. */
    }
    <nav class="toc" id="toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <div class="toc-inner">
        <span class="toc-label">On this page</span>
        <div class="toc-scroll">
          <div class="toc-list">
            {(hasBlocks ? derivedTocLinks : tocLinks).map((l) => <a href={l.href}>{l.label}</a>)}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </nav>

    {
      /* ---------- WHAT (charcoal) — definition + image ----------

       THE MIGRATION SWITCH (docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md 2.3) starts here. `hasBlocks`
       means "this build can draw this page from its blocks" — not "WordPress
       sent rows" (src/lib/page-content.ts). When it is true PageBlocks draws
       the eight bands in the order an editor arranged them; when it is false —
       which is every build until the backfill has run, and again the moment an
       editor empties the field — every band below renders exactly as it always
       has.

       Written as one guard per band rather than one guard around all eight on
       purpose, and it is not a style choice. Astro's compressHTML pass treats
       an Astro comment expression differently inside a component slot than at
       element level: wrapping these bands in a Fragment silently drops one
       space at every comment boundary below, and scripts/vr-html.mjs compares
       exact bytes and calls that a difference. A guard sitting exactly where
       its section sat emits byte-identical HTML.

       EVERY top-level band between here and <VirtualConsult> is guarded, and
       every one of them has a row in cms/import/block-map.json. Those two facts
       have to move together: a band that is neither blocked nor guarded renders
       in ADDITION to the block run and lands at the bottom of the page carrying
       a duplicate id, and a band that is guarded with no map row disappears on
       backfill with nothing in the log. There is no third outcome. The bands
       ABOVE this line — the breadcrumb, the hero, `.keys` and the rail — are
       not blocks by spec 7 and are deliberately unguarded, as are
       <VirtualConsult> and <FinalBand> below the run.

       CHARCOAL, NOT CREAM. This comment used to say "(cream)". `.section.alt`
       is `var(--vs-charcoal-green)` on THIS sheet (gum-contouring.css:245),
       with h2 #fff at :249 and p rgba(255,255,255,0.85) at :251. It is cream on
       teeth-whitening.css, which is a different page and a different token. */
    }
    {hasBlocks ? <PageBlocks blocks={blocks} /> : <section class="section alt" id="what" data-vs-reveal-batch>
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="what-grid">
          <div class="what-img" data-vs-parallax data-parallax="-25">
            <Image
              src={image("whatImg").url} width={image("whatImg").width} height={image("whatImg").height}
              alt="Close-up of a smile showing the gum line framing the upper teeth"
              widths={[500, 800, 1100]}
              sizes="(max-width: 991px) 100vw, 50vw"
            />
          </div>
          <div class="what-text">
            <span class="eyebrow">{section("what").eyebrow}</span>
            <h2><Fragment set:html={section("what").heading} /></h2>
            <div class="prose">
              <p>{section("what").body}</p>
            </div>
            <div class="cta-row left">
              <div class="cta-buttons">
                <Button variant="light" href="#consult" hoverLabel="Get a Video">Free Virtual Consult</Button>
                <Button variant="ghost-light" href="#process" hoverLabel="View Steps">How It Works</Button>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>}

    {/* ---------- LASER (sage-pale) — Natural-with-quote pattern, repainted on
         the `.section.dark` band. Holds the strongest pull-quote moment in the
         copy. SAGE-PALE, not sage: `.section.dark` is var(--vs-sage-pale) on
         this sheet (gum-contouring.css:254), with h2 var(--vs-ink) at :258.
         `sage` and `sage-pale` are two separate BLOCK_BANDS choices painting
         two different tokens, so the map says sage-pale. */}
    {hasBlocks ? null : <section class="section dark" id="laser" data-vs-reveal-batch>
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="natural-grid">
          <div class="natural-text">
            <span class="eyebrow light">Laser Technology</span>
            <h2><Fragment set:html={section("laser").heading} /></h2>
            <div class="natural-quote">
              Most patients don't realize the procedure actually happened until they look in the mirror.
              The laser is that gentle.
            </div>
            <span class="natural-quote-attrib">— Dr. Bryce Richardson, DDS, Vivid Smiles Dentistry</span>
            <div class="prose">
              <p>{section("laser").body}</p>
              <p>
                The precision of laser technology also means more predictable results and less post-procedure
                sensitivity. Dr. Richardson uses laser gum reshaping as the standard approach for all contouring
                work at Vivid Smiles precisely because of that consistency.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="natural-img" data-vs-parallax data-parallax="-25">
            <Image
              src={image("gumlineImg").url} width={image("gumlineImg").width} height={image("gumlineImg").height}
              alt="Side-lit macro close-up of lips and upper teeth"
              widths={[500, 800, 1100]}
              sizes="(max-width: 991px) 100vw, 50vw"
            />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>}

    {/* ---------- CANDIDATES (paper) — Why-grid 2-card pattern.
         Card 1: "Who Benefits…" paragraph; Card 2: "Smile Makeover" sub-section.
         Paper because `.gcontouring .section` (gum-contouring.css:244) sets
         padding and nothing else — no background, so the body paper shows. */}
    {hasBlocks ? null : <section class="section" id="candidates" data-vs-reveal-batch>
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <span class="eyebrow">{section("candidates").eyebrow}</span>
          <h2><Fragment set:html={section("candidates").heading} /></h2>
          <p>{section("candidates").body}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="why-grid">
          <article class="why-card">
            <div class="num">01</div>
            <h3>Strong-Fit Candidates</h3>
            <p class="lead">A balanced gum-to-tooth ratio is something whitening or alignment alone cannot reach.</p>
            <p>
              Many candidates have spent years hiding their smile in photos or feeling self-conscious about their
              gum-to-tooth ratio, even after investing in other cosmetic work.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="why-card">
            <div class="num">02</div>
            <h3>Gum Contouring as Part of a Complete Smile Makeover</h3>
            <p class="lead">Reshaping the gum line first ensures every restoration sits on a balanced, well-proportioned base.</p>
            <p>
              Gum contouring enhances <a class="vs-link" href="/cosmetic-dentistry/porcelain-veneers/">porcelain veneer</a> outcomes by establishing the ideal tooth proportions and symmetry
              that veneers are then designed to match. When gum tissue is uneven, veneers built over that
              foundation will look uneven too.
            </p>
            <p>
              Dr. Richardson uses digital smile design to show patients a preview of their projected outcome
              before any work begins, so the plan is always something patients have seen, understood, and agreed
              to. That preview step is something most practices in the area do not offer, and it removes the fear
              of committing to a result you have not yet seen.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>}

    {/* ---------- PROCESS (sage-pale) — 5-step grid + dark inline CTA.
         Same `.section.dark` rule as `laser`, gum-contouring.css:254. ---------- */}
    {hasBlocks ? null : <section class="section dark" id="process" data-vs-reveal-batch>
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <span class="eyebrow light">The Process</span>
          <h2><Fragment set:html={section("process").heading} /></h2>
          <p>{section("process").body}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="process-grid">
          {processSteps.map((s) => (
            <div class="process-card">
              <span class="step-tag">{s.tag}</span>
              <div class="num">{s.num}</div>
              <h3>{s.title}</h3>
              <p>{s.body}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <div class="inline-cta dark">
          <div class="ic-text">Ready for the next step? <em>Book online and we'll take it from here.</em></div>
          <div class="ic-buttons">
            <Button variant="light" href={bookNowHref} hoverLabel="Let's Talk">Book Online</Button>
            <Button variant="ghost-light" href={phoneHref} hoverLabel="Tap to Dial">Call {phoneLabel}</Button>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>}

    {/* ---------- RECOVERY (paper) — Lasting 2-col with stat ---------- */}
    {hasBlocks ? null : <section class="section" id="recovery" data-vs-reveal-batch>
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <span class="eyebrow">Recovery</span>
          <h2><Fragment set:html={section("recovery").heading} /></h2>
          <p>{section("recovery").body}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="lasting-card">
          <div class="lasting-stat">
            <div class="big">1–3<em>days</em></div>
            <div class="label">Typical sensitivity window</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lasting-body">
            <p>
              Expect minor sensitivity for one to three days, manageable with over-the-counter pain relief. Soft
              foods are recommended for a day or two, along with gentle brushing around the healing area and
              brief avoidance of spicy or acidic foods.
            </p>
            <p>
              The gum contour continues to settle over the first week or two, with final results fully apparent
              within that window.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>}

    {/* ---------- RESULTS (charcoal) — Natural-style 2-col, image + text. Uses
         alt-flip so the image lands on the left and the text on the right,
         giving the page rhythm a different shape from the Laser section above.

         CHARCOAL: the same `.section.alt` rule as `what`, gum-contouring.css:245.
         The old "(cream)" here was read off the class name rather than the rule.

         `.alt-flip` is a page-local mirror (gum-contouring.css:541-546: columns
         1.1fr 1fr, `.natural-img { order: 0 }`, `.natural-text { order: 1 }`),
         and media_split ties its class family to `media_side` — left is the
         `.what-*` family, right is the `.natural-*` family. So the backfilled
         band keeps the photo on the left and keeps the 1.1fr/1fr split, as
         `.what-grid.ratio-wide-media`, and loses the 4/5 plate. Recorded as a
         known_gap on that block in the map. ---------- */}
    {hasBlocks ? null : <section class="section alt" id="results" data-vs-reveal-batch>
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="natural-grid alt-flip">
          <div class="natural-img" data-vs-parallax data-parallax="-25">
            <Image
              src={image("resultsImg").url} width={image("resultsImg").width} height={image("resultsImg").height}
              alt="Smiling woman photographed inside the Vivid Smiles Dentistry office in Parker, CO"
              widths={[500, 800, 1100]}
              sizes="(max-width: 991px) 100vw, 50vw"
            />
          </div>
          <div class="natural-text">
            <span class="eyebrow">Results</span>
            <h2><Fragment set:html={section("results").heading} /></h2>
            <div class="prose">
              <p>{section("results").body}</p>
              <p>
                Results are permanent: reshaped gum tissue does not regenerate back to its original position.
                Dr. Richardson's approach to reshaping is grounded in natural proportions rather than maximizing
                exposure. The goal is a balance that fits the patient's face, tooth structure, and overall smile
                goals. With 600+ hours of specialized esthetic training and digital smile design integrated into
                every cosmetic case, outcomes are planned and communicated before treatment begins.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>}

    {/* ---------- WHY (paper) — 3-up 6-card grid. Substitutes for the veneers
         template's 2-card .why-grid because the "Why Choose Vivid Smiles"
         copy expands into six bullets that each warrant a tagged card.

         The comment above is what the band was planned as; the band that ships
         is TWO cards in a TWO-column grid — `.gcontouring .why-3up` is
         `repeat(2, 1fr)` (gum-contouring.css:381) and the CMS `whyCards` group
         holds two rows. card_grid draws `.why-grid > article.why-card` with the
         card's `meta` in a `.num` div, so the backfilled band keeps the copy and
         the two-column split and loses the `.why-3up` chrome and the `.tag`
         span. The closing `.cta-row` has no field on the layout at all.
         Both are known_gaps on that block in the map. ---------- */}
    {hasBlocks ? null : <section class="section" id="why" data-vs-reveal-batch>
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <span class="eyebrow">{section("why").eyebrow}</span>
          <h2><Fragment set:html={section("why").heading} /></h2>
          <p>{section("why").body}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="why-3up">
          {whyCards.map((c) => (
            <article class="why-3up-card">
              <span class="tag">{c.tag}</span>
              <h3>{c.title}</h3>
              <p>{c.body}</p>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>

        <div class="cta-row">
          <div class="cta-buttons">
            <Button variant="sage" href={bookNowHref} hoverLabel="Book Now">Book Online</Button>
            <Button variant="ghost" href={directionsHref} hoverLabel="Open Map">Get Directions</Button>
          </div>
          <span class="cta-note">17167 Cedar Gulch Pkwy Ste 102 · Parker, CO</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>}

    {/* ---------- FAQ (paper) — native details/summary, zero JS ---------- */}
    {hasBlocks ? null : <section class="section" id="faq" data-vs-reveal-batch>
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <span class="eyebrow">{section("faq").eyebrow}</span>
          <h2><Fragment set:html={section("faq").heading} /></h2>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-list">
          {faqs.map((f) => (
            <details class="faq" open={f.open}>
              <summary><h3>{f.q}</h3><span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
              <div class="answer">{f.a}</div>
            </details>
          ))}
        </div>

        <div class="pull">
          <p>{section("faq").body}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="cta-row">
          <div class="cta-buttons">
            <Button variant="sage" href="#consult" hoverLabel="Get a Video">Still Have Questions? Free Virtual Consult</Button>
            <Button variant="ghost" href={phoneHref} hoverLabel="Tap to Dial">Call {phoneLabel}</Button>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>}

    {/* ---------- VIRTUAL CONSULT — primary closing conversion. Anchor #consult
         is the destination for every mid-page CTA on this page.
         NOT a block and deliberately unguarded, like the breadcrumb, hero,
         `.keys` strip and the rail above: it renders on both sides of the switch
         and has no row in the map. It sits after the block run in template order
         and PageBlocks draws at the `what` position, so the closing pair still
         comes last. */}
    <VirtualConsult id="consult" eyebrow="Free gum contouring consultation">
      <Fragment slot="headline">Free gum contouring <em>consultation.</em></Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="body">
        Gum contouring is one of the most efficient cosmetic procedures available: immediate results, minimal
        recovery, and a lasting change to the way your smile looks. If you have ever wondered whether your gum
        line is affecting the way your teeth appear, a consultation with Dr. Richardson will give you a clear
        answer and a preview of what is possible before any commitment is made.
      </Fragment>
    </VirtualConsult>

    {/* ---------- CLOSING BAND ---------- */}
    <FinalBand>
      <p>Free cosmetic consultations at 17167 Cedar Gulch Pkwy, Suite 102, Parker, CO 80134.</p>
    </FinalBand>

    </main>

